   121	The bound is a screening result, not a closed-loop guarantee.
   122	
   123	---
   124	
   125	## P3. Routing cannot win under nonpositive System-2 advantage
   126	
   127	**Proposition 3** (one-step routing model). Fix a state distribution ρ,
   128	a System-1 action map `a₁(s)`, a System-2 action map `a₂(s)`, and a
   129	per-call cost `c ≥ 0`. A router is a measurable set `S₂` of states on
   130	which System-2 is consulted. With matched one-step continuation, define
   131	the per-state net advantage `A(s) = Q(s, a₂(s)) − Q(s, a₁(s))`. Then the
   132	router's value over never routing is
   133	
   134	```
   135	V(S₂) = E[ 1{s ∈ S₂} · A(s) ] − c · ρ(S₂).
   136	```
   137	
   138	If `A(s) ≤ 0` ρ-almost surely, then `V(S₂) ≤ 0` for **every** router —
   139	oracle or learned, confidence-based or not — with strict inequality
   140	unless `S₂` avoids all states where `A(s) < 0`.
   141	
   142	**Proof.** Both terms are nonpositive term-by-term. ∎
   143	
   144	**Corollary (frontier degeneracy).** On a panel where the System-2's
   145	per-state advantage is nonpositive, no routing design can recover value;
   146	the cost frontier is degenerate at "System-1 only". Routing research on
   147	such a panel can only lose.
   148	
   149	**Empirical reading.** The 10-seed EXP-RQ3 sweep measured eight
   150	LLM-calling arms (always-LLM, four confidence thresholds, three random
   151	rates); none beat `rf` with a CI excluding zero on any family, and
   152	always-LLM lost detectably on three families. Under the one-step model
   153	this pattern is CONSISTENT WITH `A(s) ≤ 0` across visited states — but
   154	the sweep measures aggregate routing outcomes and cannot establish the
   155	pointwise premise directly. `A(s) ≤ 0` is an unmeasured premise, not a
   156	quantity the sweep estimated: the proposition says what FOLLOWS if the
   157	premise holds; the sweep provides the conditional's empirical
   158	illustration, not its antecedent's verification. Two supplementary observations fit: (a) the
   159	calibrated RF softmax maximum rarely exceeds 0.25 with m = 8 (near the
   160	uniform 0.125), so confidence thresholds τ ≥ 0.25 all induce the *same*
   161	router (route-always); the tested router family collapses to its two
   162	extremes, and the random-rate arms confirm the interior points also lose.
   163	(b) The deterministic schedule control (below) shows the LLM's decisions
   164	are dominated even by a fixed two-phase schedule — additional evidence
   165	CONSISTENT WITH `A ≤ 0` (not a verification of the pointwise premise).
   166	
   167	**Caveat.** `V` is additive only in the one-step model with matched
   168	continuation. Sequential composition breaks additivity (Phase 8.6F), so
   169	the closed-loop statement is verified empirically per-arm rather than
   170	deduced. Read conditionally: IF the pointwise premise held on this
   171	panel, P3 would imply the same outcome for any untested router — but
   172	the premise is unmeasured, so this is a conditional statement about the
   173	sweep's explanation, not a prediction about untested routers.
   174	
